<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Đakovo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Đakovo is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> region of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, seat of a Catholic bishopric since the 13th century. Its large neo-Romanesque cathedral of St Peter was built between 1866 and 1882 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-juraj-strossmayer">Josip Juraj Strossmayer</a>. The town is also known for its stud farm of Lipizzaner horses.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dakovo.jpg" alt="Đakovo" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nick Savchenko from Kiev, Ukraine · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dakovo_(19677163348).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Đakovo is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, 37 km south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osijek">Osijek</a> and 34 km south-east of Našice, at an elevation of 111 m, and it is the centre of the fertile Đakovo region. The name comes from the Greek diákos, in Slavic form đak, 'pupil'; it is uncertain whether it came directly from Greek, from Hungarian diák or from the local Slavic form. In Roman antiquity the settlement of Certissia stood on the same spot until it disappeared in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/migration-period">Migration Period</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The settlement is first mentioned in 1239, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-iv-of-hungary">Béla IV of Hungary</a> granted it to the Diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> moved his seat there in 1246. A predecessor of the present St Peter's Cathedral was built in 1355. In 1374 the place is recorded as Dyacou, and on 25 July 1386 Croatian rebels captured Queen Mary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and her mother Elizabeth near it. Ottoman rule began after the Battle of Gorjani in 1537 and lasted nearly 150 years, during which Đakovo, then called Yakova, was the centre of a kaza in the Sanjak of Pojega.</p><p>In 1805 a herd of Lipizzan horses was evacuated to Đakovo when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and part of it stayed. In 1910 the population of 6,304 comprised 4,894 Croatians, 890 Germans, 249 Hungarians and 164 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>. Around then the town was a district capital in Virovitica County in the Kingdom of Croatia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a>. From 1 December 1941 until 7 July 1942 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustaše</a> operated the Đakovo internment camp, mostly for Jewish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a> and Serb women and children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Đakovo has 19,491 inhabitants in the town itself, and its economy rests on farming, livestock, leather and wool processing, wood processing and food production. The Cathedral basilica of St Peter, built in 1866–1882 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-juraj-strossmayer">Josip Juraj Strossmayer</a>, then Catholic bishop of Đakovo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrmia">Srijem</a>, is the most important sacral building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a>. A park of the 19th century near the bishop's palace is a protected horticultural monument. The folklore festival Đakovački vezovi is held every year at the beginning of July, with traditional costumes, dances and a horse and wedding wagon show, and white Lipizzaner horses from the breeding centre at Ivandvor, active since 1506, can be seen on the racecourse. The region is also known for its Slavonian cooking and wines.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Đakovo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Osijek's fortress, Tvrđa, was rebuilt in the 1710s and 1720s, and the bishopric of <b>Đakovo</b> revived Catholic life, while Orthodox Serbs were organised around Sremski Karlovci.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-slavonia">Kingdom of Slavonia</a></li></ul></section>
